another Wisconsin community is reeling  after the second incident this week  where police shot an armed student on a  high school campus the latest violence  unfolded yesterday morning in Oshkosh  officials say a 16 year old student was  wounded after stabbing a school resource  officer on Monday around 70 miles away  in Waukesha police shot a 17 year old  who they say threatened an officer with  a pellet gun Dean Reynolds has been  following both of these incidents the  first emergency calls from Oshkosh West  High School came shortly after 9:00 a.m.  we'll say a 16 year old student stabbed  a school resource officer in his office  on campus at which time the officer  fired on the student striking student  one time life-saving measures were  provided to both the officer and the  suspect in this incident both were  hospitalized students were evacuated  from school and later reunited with  their parents I actually got a text from  him that said I'm okay he said I have to  stay silent now the police are sweeping  the school  it's the second Wisconsin shooting in  two days involving law enforcement  Monday and Waukesha police say a 17 year  old student pointed this pellet gun at a  fellow student who then went for help  police had limited information about the  suspects motive he was angry he was  angry angry at another student police  say he then threatened a responding  officer and that's when 11 year veteran  sergeant Brady Esser shot the suspect  once in the leg and twice in the arm he  was put in handcuffs as officials  applied tourniquets to stop the bleeding  they later found another pellet gun in  the students backpack there could be  multiple felony charges on this incident  officials confirm that all public  schools here will be closed for the day  for CBS this morning Dean Reynolds  Oshkosh Wisconsin
 

